Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether a graded ring homomorphism that is a linear isomorphism in degrees ≤ k_m must be a ring automorphism, given that the cohomology is a polynomial algebra with generators in degrees k_1, ..., k_m ≤ k_m.

**Answer: Yes, f\* is a ring automorphism.**

## Proof

Let $H = \widetilde{H}^*(X;\,\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}) \cong \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}[x_1,\dots,x_m]$ with $|x_i|=k_i$ and $k_1\le\cdots\le k_m$. Since all generators lie in positive degree, $X$ is connected and $H$ is a connected graded algebra (with unit adjoined in degree 0).

**Key observations:**

**(1) f\* is a graded ring homomorphism.** Any continuous map induces a graded ring homomorphism on cohomology. So $f^*(ab)=f^*(a)\,f^*(b)$ and $f^*$ preserves degree.

**(2) f\* preserves decomposables.** Let $D^d \subseteq H^d$ denote the subspace of *decomposable* elements—the image of the multiplication map
$$\mu_d:\bigoplus_{\substack{i+j=d\\i,j>0}} H^i\otimes H^j \longrightarrow H^d.$$
Since $f^*$ is a ring homomorphism, $f^*(D^d)\subseteq D^d$ for every $d$.

**(3) f\* induces an isomorphism on indecomposables in degrees $\le k_m$.** Define the indecomposables $QH^d = H^d/D^d$. By hypothesis, $f^*:H^d\to H^d$ is a linear isomorphism for $d\le k_m$. Since $f^*(D^d)\subseteq D^d$ and $f^*$ is injective on $H^d$, we get $\dim f^*(D^d)=\dim D^d$, hence $f^*(D^d)=D^d$. Therefore $f^*$ descends to an isomorphism $Qf^*:QH^d\to QH^d$ for every $d\le k_m$.

**(4) Qf\* is an isomorphism on all of $QH$.** The indecomposables of the polynomial algebra $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}[x_1,\dots,x_m]$ are spanned by (the images of) $x_1,\dots,x_m$ in degrees $k_1,\dots,k_m$. Since every $k_i\le k_m$, the indecomposables are concentrated in degrees $\le k_m$. By step (3), $Qf^*$ is an isomorphism in every degree where $QH$ is nonzero.

**(5) Inductive lifting: a graded ring endomorphism that is an isomorphism on indecomposables is a ring isomorphism.**

We prove by induction on $d$ that $f^*:H^d\to H^d$ is an isomorphism.

*Base case ($d=0$):* $H^0=\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$ and $f^*(1)=1$, so $f^*$ is an isomorphism.

*Inductive step:* Assume $f^*$ is an isomorphism on $H^i$ for all $i<d$. Consider the commutative diagram with exact rows:
$$\begin{array}{ccccccc}
0&\to&D^d&\to&H^d&\to&QH^d&\to&0\\
&&\downarrow{\scriptstyle f^*}&&\downarrow{\scriptstyle f^*}&&\downarrow{\scriptstyle Qf^*}\\
0&\to&D^d&\to&H^d&\to&QH^d&\to&0
\end{array}$$

- **$f^*$ is an isomorphism on $D^d$:** By the induction hypothesis, $f^*$ is an isomorphism on each $H^i$ for $i<d$, so $f^*\otimes f^*$ is an isomorphism on $\bigoplus_{i+j=d} H^i\otimes H^j$. Since $f^*\circ\mu_d=\mu_d\circ(f^*\otimes f^*)$ and $f^*\otimes f^*$ is an isomorphism on the source, we get $f^*(D^d)=f^*(\operatorname{im}\mu_d)=\operatorname{im}\mu_d=D^d$. Each graded piece $H^d$ is finite-dimensional (the polynomial ring in finitely many generators of positive degree has finitely many monomials in each degree), so $f^*|_{D^d}:D^d\to D^d$ is a surjective endomorphism of a finite-dimensional space, hence an isomorphism.

- **$Qf^*$ is an isomorphism on $QH^d$:** This is step (4).

By the Five Lemma (or direct diagram chase), $f^*:H^d\to H^d$ is an isomorphism.

By induction, $f^*$ is an isomorphism in every degree, hence $f^*:H\to H$ is a ring automorphism. $\blacksquare$

